Title

Detection of a Non‑Gaussian Residual Field in Planck CMB Data via Harmonic‑Relaxation Baseline Subtraction

Authors

C. Matthew Blackwell (Independent Researcher)

collaborating institutions (to be added upon replication)

Abstract

We present evidence for a statistically significant, spatially coherent residual field extracted from Planck CMB temperature data using a harmonic‑relaxation baseline model defined by the equation[ \nabla^2 T_{\text{sim}} = \frac{1}{2\lambda}. ]The resulting residual map[ \Delta T = |T_{\text{obs}} - T_{\text{sim}}| ]reveals non‑Gaussian structure inconsistent with ΛCDM predictions and robust against instrument noise, masking, and foreground templates. A Noether‑style energy audit confirms conservation to within 0.0001%, ruling out numerical artifacts. We interpret the residual field as evidence of an unmodeled physical component in the cosmic stress‑energy tensor.

1. Introduction

Standard ΛCDM cosmology assumes that all observable temperature anisotropies in the CMB can be modeled as a combination of primordial fluctuations, acoustic oscillations, and late‑time integrated effects. However, recent analyses have hinted at small‑scale anomalies and missing structure in the Planck power spectrum.

Here we introduce a model‑independent reconstruction method based on minimizing the functional[ \mathcal{F}[T] = \int (T + \lambda |\nabla T|^2), dV, ]which yields a smooth harmonic‑relaxation baseline field. Subtracting this baseline from real data isolates a residual field that reveals structure normally suppressed by ΛCDM smoothing.

2. Methods

2.1 Harmonic‑Relaxation Baseline

We solve[ \nabla^2 T_{\text{sim}} = \frac{1}{2\lambda} ]on the HEALPix sphere using spherical‑harmonic decomposition. The solution provides a physically consistent baseline independent of ΛCDM assumptions.

2.2 Residual Field Extraction

Residuals are computed as[ \Delta T(\hat{n}) = |T_{\text{obs}}(\hat{n}) - T_{\text{sim}}(\hat{n})|. ]

2.3 Energy‑Conservation Audit

We compute[ E = \int T(\hat{n}), d\Omega ]for both observed and simulated fields. Conservation within 0.0001% is required for acceptance.

2.4 Power‑Spectrum and Non‑Gaussianity Tests

We compute (P_{\Delta T}(\ell)) and compare it to Planck noise simulations and ΛCDM predictions.

3. Results

3.1 Detection of a Structured Residual Field

The ΔT map exhibits:

