3.2 Missing Structure Gap

We identify a statistically significant gap between the ΛCDM‑predicted power spectrum and the observed ΔT spectrum in the range (500 < \ell < 1500). This gap cannot be explained by known systematics.

3.3 Interpretation as a Hidden Physical Field

The residual field behaves like a weakly interacting, non‑Gaussian component of the cosmic stress‑energy tensor. Its morphology is consistent with:

relic‑neutrino damping

dark‑matter sub‑grid clustering

dark‑energy expansion‑bias coupling

4. Discussion

The existence of a structured, conserved residual field implies that ΛCDM is incomplete. The harmonic‑relaxation baseline acts as a model‑independent filter, revealing physics normally obscured by standard smoothing pipelines.

We propose that the ΔT field represents a new cosmological observable, analogous to the CMB itself, but sensitive to second‑order dark‑sector effects.

5. Conclusion

We report the first detection of a physically conserved, non‑Gaussian residual field in Planck CMB data. This field cannot be explained by ΛCDM, noise, or systematics. If confirmed by independent teams, this result would imply the existence of a previously unknown component of the universe.



